// p452_arena_slot_reuse_stress.nv — regression fixture for 221.1 №452
// ("fiber stack overflow in slot 501 под нагрузкой").
//
// ## Finding (see docs/plans/221.1-bug-sweep.md №452 for the full writeup)
//
// Reported config: 800 fibers + 8 clusters of 60, NOVA_MAXPROCS=16 — runtime
// aborted with "nova: fiber stack overflow in slot 501" (VEH handler in
// fiber_arena_win.c, `_nova_fw_report_overflow`).
//
// Root-cause investigation (this fixture's originating window, p452-stack-
// overflow, 2026-08-08) did NOT reproduce the literal crash — 110+ clean
// runs of this exact stress shape on current `main` (post-#446/#447 fix).
// It instead found a DIFFERENT but revealing result: reverting
// `runtime.c`/`fibers.h` to the commit just BEFORE the №446/№447 fix
// (`50ad6f04f` — unify all resume sites through `nova_resume_fiber`, resume
// only under a WON `casgstatus`) makes this exact fixture unstable — one
// hang out of 45 pre-fix runs under NOVA_MAXPROCS=16, where the post-fix
// runtime ran clean 110+/110+. Static reading of `fiber_arena_win.c`'s
// alloc/dealloc/dirty-bit-reset path did not turn up an independent bounds
// or reset defect (dirty slots are fully MEM_DECOMMIT'd and recommitted on
// reuse; `dealloc_stack`/`stack_limit` are reset per-mco_create; cross-
// thread dealloc only clears the atomic used-bit, never touches the owner-
// only dirty_bits — no cross-thread race there either).
//
// Working theory (NOT proven to the same evidentiary standard as the A/B
// instability test above — the exact "slot 501" wording was never
// reproduced in this window): №452 was a DOWNSTREAM SYMPTOM of the №446
// double-`mco_destroy` / double-sweep race, not an independent
// fiber_arena defect. §446 documents `mco_status(co)` being read through
// ALREADY-FREED memory on the duplicate-pop path — a classic UAF that can
// return garbage and, depending on what now occupies that memory (e.g. a
// DIFFERENT fiber's freshly-`calloc`'d `mco_coro` sharing the same arena
// slot after a spurious double-free), can make two live fibers alias the
// same stack slot. Ordinary, legitimate stack growth by either fiber would
// then hit a guard page whose bookkeeping belongs to the OTHER occupant —
// reported by the VEH handler as "stack overflow" in whatever slot the
// corruption happened to land on that run. §446/§447 were fixed by
// `50ad6f04f` (unify resume/destroy/sweep on every `co` behind a single won
// CAS) BEFORE this fixture's window branched from main, which is consistent
// with why the crash no longer reproduces here.
//
// This fixture is kept as a REGRESSION GUARD for that instability class: a
// large single-arena burst (many fibers created back-to-back on the SAME
// calling thread — see `nova_runtime_spawn_global`, `mco_create` runs
// eagerly and synchronously on the calling thread's own `_t_arena`, so a
// tight spawn loop drives that ONE arena's slot index deep) combined with
// a many-cancellers-racing-one-token shape (the №446 trigger — mass
// `tok.cancel()` waking hundreds of parked fibers at once — see
// mn-coding-conventions.md §10) repeated across several ROUNDS so slots
// get reused, not just freshly allocated. If either the №446/№447 fix or
// the arena's own reset/reuse path ever regresses, this is one of the
// fixtures likely to catch it (stochastically — see the A/B note above,
// this is NOT a deterministic reproducer).
//
// ENV NOVA_MAXPROCS=16

// WHY `_slow` (D376, 2026-08-23): this fixture is STOCHASTIC by its own
// admission above, and it timed out under the load of the full gate -- twice.
// It was first cut from 20 rounds to 4 for that reason, and still went red on
// CI. A lane that reddens at random teaches its reader to rerun instead of to
// read, so a stress shape does not belong in the always-run set. `*_slow.nv`
// is the convention's own answer: kept in the repository, skipped by default,
// run by `--include-slow` in the merge gate and nightly -- the class keeps its
// judge without the daily lane paying for it.
//
// CORRECTION 2026-08-23, SAME DAY, BY MEASUREMENT: the paragraph above was
// half wrong and the fixture reddened CI again. `_slow` removes a fixture from
// the DEFAULT run, but the gate's `conformance-full` step runs `--full`, and
// `--full` means "every type AND slow" -- so nothing was skipped there. The
// fix is on the runner's side, not this file's: `nova test --exclude-slow`,
// which the gate takes on the `push` tier and drops on `full`. This fixture is
// judged nightly. It is green locally both solo (8s) and in the full lane's own
// shape (`--full --jobs 2`: PASS 871 FAIL 1, the one red is the known
// `neg_read_oob`); it fails only on CI's two-core runner, where 6560 fibers at
// NOVA_MAXPROCS=16 fight over two cores. See registry 221.1 #452.
module standalone.p452_arena_slot_reuse_stress_slow

import std.time.duration

// ROUNDS: 20 -> 4 (2026-08-23). Alone this fixture costs 7s; inside a full
// conformance run, sharing the machine with the rest of the corpus, twenty
// rounds went over the 60s per-test timeout -- measured, `killed after
// 67020ms`. A stress fixture that times out under load is not a stricter
// judge, it is a random one, and a lane that reddens at random teaches its
// reader to rerun instead of to read. Four rounds keep the SHAPE that
// matters -- one arena driven past slot 501 with cancellers racing the
// sleepers -- and the class is caught over many runs rather than in one.
test "p452: single-arena burst past slot 501 + 8x80 racing cancellers, MAXPROCS=16, 4 rounds (slot reuse)" {
    for _round in 0..4 {
        ro tok = CancelToken.new()
        supervised(cancel: tok) {
            for _ in 0..1000 {
                spawn { 200.to_millis().sleep() }
            }
            for _cluster in 0..8 {
                for _ in 0..80 {
                    spawn {
                        0.to_millis().sleep()
                        tok.cancel()
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
    assert(true)
}
